WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.980
A legal, honest label, and proof that your packaging works.

2
00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:08.550
This is the video for Module 3 of Product Packaging and Branding.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:22.980
NAFDAC regulates processed food, drinks and cosmetics. To sell through shops and supermarkets, you need registration and a label that meets its rules. Always check the current guidelines for your product.

4
00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:32.600
Your label needs the product name on the front, and the ingredients from largest to smallest, with allergens like groundnuts named clearly.

5
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:41.380
Net content in metric units, such as 300 g. And your business name and full address, not just a phone number.

6
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:51.140
Dates written day, month, full year, with a batch number, so each production run can be traced. And your NAFDAC number, exactly as issued.

7
00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:55.160
If any box is unticked, the label is not ready for shops.

8
00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:06.540
For example, Chika in Enugu wants a supermarket to stock her pepper sauce. Her label says Exp 12/26, has no weight, and only a phone number.

9
00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:17.650
She writes Date of Manufacture and Best Before with the full year, adds net weight 300 g on the front, lists her ingredients, and prints her full address.

10
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.570
She has no NAFDAC number yet, so she does not print one. She starts registration instead.

11
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:33.900
Never print a NAFDAC number you have not been issued. A made-up, borrowed or expected number can lead to seizure of your goods and prosecution.

12
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:42.790
Never make medical claims. For example, Hauwa's first shea butter label said cures eczema. She removed it completely.

13
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:53.900
And never copy the colours, layout or name style of a famous product. Build your own look, then protect it with CAC registration and a trademark.

14
00:01:55.150 --> 00:02:09.160
Every claim must be true and something you could prove. Hauwa had no organic certificate, so organic came off. Chemical-free is never true, because water and salt are chemicals. And best in Africa cannot be proven.

15
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:22.510
She replaced them with honest, specific words: no added fragrance, which is true, and hand-whipped in Kaduna from unrefined shea butter. Her label is shorter, honest, and still appealing.

16
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.470
Use large, plain fonts and strong contrast, so older buyers can read.

17
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.530
Show the product type and size in words and a simple picture, for buyers who read little English.

18
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.770
Add one short line in a local language if your buyers use it, keeping English as the main language.

19
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:56.460
And use only as much packaging as protection needs. If you reuse returned glass jars, check for chips and cracks, wash in hot soapy water, sterilise and dry fully before refilling.

20
00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:07.910
Now prove it works. First record a baseline, your starting numbers, for at least four weeks with the old packaging. Then measure the same things for four weeks after.

21
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:21.550
With old nylon bags, Amaka sold 200 units at 500 naira. That is 100,000 naira. 12 were returned soft or torn, a damage rate of 6%.

22
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:34.340
With sealed pouches, she sold 240 units at 700 naira. That is 168,000 naira, and only 2 returns, about 0.8%.

23
00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:47.090
The better packaging cost her 24,000 naira more. But after all costs, her profit went from about 30,000 to about 60,000 naira, and two shops now stock her.

24
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.390
She also notes that December demand might have helped. Be honest about other causes.

25
00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.010
Write it on one page. What you changed, with two photos.

26
00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.350
Why, meaning the problem you found.

27
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.460
Your before and after numbers, in a small table.

28
00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:08.230
Anything else that might have moved the numbers.

29
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:15.000
And what you will keep, change and measure next. A reader should understand it in under two minutes.

30
00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:27.090
To remember. Complete your label and write dates clearly. Never fake a NAFDAC number. Make only claims you can prove. Measure before and after. And report honestly.

31
00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:36.870
Now audit your label against the checklist, review every claim and make version 3, and set up your packaging scorecard with your baseline numbers.

32
00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.170
Continue with the lessons below.
